  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers pay for people who can contribute early. They judge a resume by clarity, proof and fit with the notice.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is writing one proof line with a number.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and career objectives for business roles: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools draft objectives that sound the same. Generic text becomes common and is easy to spot.
  </p>
  <p>
    A specific proof line and honest fit hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI for first drafts; later, rewrite with your own proof.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general career objectives for business roles path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Summary section</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">More room</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Less standard</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Skills-first resume</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Shows skill</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs proof</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Project-led resume</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Strong proof</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs a project</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
